00:00:44.302 --> 00:00:47.020
All right.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:51.720
So let's just quickly
recap what data pipeline is

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:52.910
and what it is for.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:58.430
As you can see,
different stages usually

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:01.820
exist in a modern data pipeline.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.909
Usually it starts
with data injection,

00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:08.450
you have to collect the
data, take the data in.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:14.240
It could be coming from
servers, users, or IOT devices

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.470
these days.

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:16.940
Once we have
collected the data, we

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:21.340
have to transform it, cleanse
it, some kind of processing.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:24.850
We then have to store the
data, [? put it ?] somewhere--

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:28.000
sometimes file systems,
storage device,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.350
or maybe in a data warehouse.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.610
And then once we have the
data in, we can take a look

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.790
and see what we can
derive from the data.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:40.360
In the old days, it could be
a simple as an aggregation,

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:43.390
summation, group by
day, group by week,

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:44.890
or maybe these
days it can be very

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:48.040
common to do some
statistical modeling

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:54.060
or maybe use it for advanced use
cases like machine learning AI.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.350
And then visualization.

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:57.960
It's a very, very
important step.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.450
Usually it is used
for presenting

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.720
the results of the analysis--

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:08.160
pie chart, bar chart,
node-link diagrams, word cloud,

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.050
you name it.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:12.420
Different types of
visualization techniques.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:16.260
Usually these stages are
done in an orchestrated,

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.530
in an automated, fashion.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:21.260
It could start with, for
example, a presence of the data

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:25.590
file, some events coming
in, some trigger, or maybe

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.140
the trigger is based
on a batch schedule.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:32.460
Although there are cases that
maybe it is done manually

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.460
during development
phase, testing phase,

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.995
or maybe it is for ad
hoc analytics project.

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:44.390
We do hear a lot of
challenges from customers

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.780
who are trying to build
data pipeline these days.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:51.780
And they are encountering
different types of things.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:56.850
Starting with injection,
there could be a huge volume

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.230
of data coming in.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:00.750
They're coming in
in different formats

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:03.060
at very, very high speed.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:04.660
And then the transformation.

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:07.240
It can be tough to
just to cater for all

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:09.570
of those requirements
that are required

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:11.790
by the downstream application.

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:14.190
You have to add in all
those transformation rules.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:17.226
You have to keep adding,
adding, adding until a point

00:03:17.226 --> 00:03:18.600
that I'm not sure
whether you all

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.956
have the experience of talking
to your data integration team.

00:03:21.956 --> 00:03:24.330
Hey, I just want to understand
how come the incoming data

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:27.690
file has a million rows and then
you're dropping 10% of them.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.300
They got rejected, they
got put somewhere else.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:31.390
What happened?

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:33.030
It's just getting
harder and harder

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:37.160
to understand what happened
in between the pipelines.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:38.900
And of the storage
itself, I mean

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.140
with the huge volume
of the storage,

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:44.300
it is really getting tough
as an exercise on its own

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.480
just to architect
the storage system.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.420
Just to make it highly
available, make it durable,

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.980
make sure that things are
backed up correctly, make sure

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:55.610
it can handle the
throughput that's coming in.

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:58.610
And then the size of
the storage will grow,

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:00.430
and you have to
figure out how to add

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:03.590
the storage in a way that
is not impacting the system

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:06.260
and then it can still catch
up with the requirements

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:08.510
of the [? IOT, ?] the
throughput, and things

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:10.770
like that.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:14.060
And then when we get to
the analysis of the data,

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:18.050
well there are many cases that
so many different users having

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:20.959
their own careers to run,
you have to figure out a way

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:22.850
to tune it for
this user and then

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:24.900
tune it for a another
group of users.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.770
And of course, with the data
volume, keep adding to it,

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:29.720
the queries may runs
slower and slower.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.460
So it's not like a
one-off exercise.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:33.150
You have to keep
monitoring it, you

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:37.850
know, all those things like
analyze it, run stats on it.

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:40.340
All those things just to
keep it up and running

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.934
and make it efficient.

00:04:42.934 --> 00:04:45.350
Well, there are some other
cases that the data is not even

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:46.183
in one single place.

00:04:46.183 --> 00:04:48.620
I mean they're in
different types of storage

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.810
in different data mounts.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:53.810
They're just in different places
making it hard to analyze.

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:55.830
And finally onto visualization.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:58.100
There are so many
cases that I've

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:00.350
seen in a customer situation.

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:02.300
They have a BI dashboard.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:05.420
But then, the caveat
is that the data

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:08.720
available for
visualization, sorry,

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:11.450
it is just the latest
three months of the data.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:14.210
If you're having good
luck in your company,

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:17.180
maybe OK, it's one year
of data, three years

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:18.790
of data in your dashboard.

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:22.460
It may not be all the data
set that you want to analyze.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:24.365
People are doing this
usually to optimize

00:05:24.365 --> 00:05:27.020
the performance of the
query so it's not overly

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:28.820
loading the dashboard.

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:31.340
To be honest in
many of the cases,

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:34.010
you don't get to run
the dashboard yourself.

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:36.220
It will be run essentially
by someone else,

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:39.680
you know, an
administrator, a server

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.900
generating the report for you.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:46.040
8:00 AM in the morning, weekly
report, they send you a PDF.

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:47.570
The PDF has a chart.

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:50.720
You detach the PDF and
that becomes your reporting

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.640
repository.

