00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:07.660
Now what are we solving
with this data pipeline?

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:09.430
Usually what we hear
from our customers

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:11.110
is that there's a
handful of things

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:14.800
that they're solving for when
it comes to building out a data

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:16.085
pipeline.

00:09:16.085 --> 00:09:17.710
One of the things
that's often the case

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:21.860
is that we want to get
timely access to the data.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:23.320
Usually people
don't want to have

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:27.760
to wait for days or even
hours to get access to data

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:31.030
to start analyzing it
and looking for insights.

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:33.700
And so we don't want to have
to wait for long, drawn out

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:36.031
ETL processes to complete
before we can actually

00:09:36.031 --> 00:09:37.030
start querying the data.

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:42.220
We may also have people
in our organization

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:45.780
who we want to enable to
make data driven decisions

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:49.790
but don't have very high
degree of technical skill.

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:54.950
They don't program in
Python or R or even SQL.

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:57.750
And so we need to enable
everyone in an organization

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:00.410
to make decisions
with data with tools

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:01.660
that are easy for them to use.

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:07.020
And finally, your data
engineers are out there

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:11.400
building the tools for these
other constituencies to use.

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:13.140
Your data engineers
actually also need

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:16.350
a way to process
data consistently,

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:18.845
and then when business rules
change or when bugs are found,

00:10:18.845 --> 00:10:20.220
they may need to
actually go back

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:23.850
and reprocess the data that
had been already processed

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:26.600
by the streaming engine.

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:29.030
Let's look at how
we solve for this.

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:33.090
This is the architecture of
the game you just played.

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:36.350
You can see that we are
going from the user--

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:39.270
that's you-- through
our application.

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:43.650
We ingest data from the
app, deliver the events

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:45.930
down to some processing
layer which prepares

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:49.090
the data for analysis.

00:10:49.090 --> 00:10:51.340
We store the data in
an analytics engine

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:52.790
where we can query
over the data,

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:55.260
build reports and dashboards.

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:58.140
And then finally, we've got
mechanisms within these tools

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:00.450
to actually share the data out.

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:03.350
So let's dive a
little bit deeper.

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:06.050
On the application,
what we did was

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:10.220
use Firebase to
implement the front end

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:11.690
that you were playing with.

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:14.750
We use Firebase Hosting for
all of the static assets,

00:11:14.750 --> 00:11:18.440
the HTML, the
JavaScript, and so forth.

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:22.210
We used Firestore to store
the questions and your answers

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:24.190
to those questions.

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:28.870
And then we use Cloud Functions
to react to the submission

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:31.540
of the answers in the app.

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:33.040
And we also use
the Cloud Function

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:36.670
to react to the updates
of the Firestore database

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:37.330
in real time.

00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:43.440
Those functions are
then emitting data

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:46.790
into a Cloud Pub/Sub topic.

00:11:46.790 --> 00:11:49.190
Cloud Pub/Sub is
a serverless way

00:11:49.190 --> 00:11:52.920
to deliver events from
an application, an IOT

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:57.740
sensor, what have you, store
it durably, and then deliver it

00:11:57.740 --> 00:11:59.638
later for processing.

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:05.370
The other side of the
coin from a Pub/Sub topic

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:06.916
is a Pub/Sub subscription.

00:12:06.916 --> 00:12:10.740
A subscription is used to
actually deliver events

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:14.090
down to interested consumers.

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:17.750
You can have up to 10,000
topics and subscriptions

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:20.180
in a GCP project.

00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:23.450
We're only using a handful
of topics and subscriptions

00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:27.480
in order to accommodate your
answers as they flow in.

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:36.720
Our consumer is a Cloud
Dataflow pipeline.

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:40.240
Dataflow is a fully managed
service for ingesting-- sorry,

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:42.690
not for ingesting but for
processing events and preparing

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:46.210
them for later analysis.

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:50.040
The data preparation may involve
just basic transformations.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:52.080
Taking the data that's
ingested-- in our case,

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:55.440
we're ingesting JSON data.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:57.090
Performing some
computation over it--

00:12:57.090 --> 00:13:01.680
we might be computing,
say, a score over the data.

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:04.290
And then storing
that into some sync--

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:06.105
in our case, we're
using BigQuery.

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:12.890
Now, if I were delivering
this talk a year ago,

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:18.020
I'd probably have a screenshot
of a snippet of some Java code

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:21.110
that was reading from
Pub/Sub, optionally doing

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:22.850
some transformation
in the middle,

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:27.380
and then storing the
data into BigQuery.

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:29.510
Not everybody's
a Java developer,

00:13:29.510 --> 00:13:31.640
but it's often the
case that we need

00:13:31.640 --> 00:13:35.300
to be able to deploy these
kinds of pipelines simply.

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:39.710
So we've recently added
to the Cloud platform

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:43.070
a number of pre-built
data flow templates

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:46.070
that allow you to easily
ingest data from a source

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:49.580
and write it to a sync
optionally transforming it

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:51.710
as it passes through
a JavaScript UVF

00:13:51.710 --> 00:13:54.280
that you can provide.

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:58.030
With these provided
templates, you

00:13:58.030 --> 00:14:01.690
can get data from Cloud
Pub/Sub into things

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:05.470
like BigQuery or Cloud
Storage without writing

00:14:05.470 --> 00:14:07.740
a single line of code.

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:11.040
In our case, what we're doing
is ingesting from Pub/Sub,

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:14.160
processing that data
minimally, and then writing it

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:15.300
into BigQuery.

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:19.620
We're also writing in an
archive out to Cloud Storage

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:22.390
as a bunch of Avro files.

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:23.970
We chose the Avro
format because it's

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:27.550
extremely well supported by all
of the other GCP components.

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:31.080
It's a first class citizen
with respect to BigQuery ingest

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:33.450
and all of the rest of
the suite of big data

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:34.620
tools on the platform.

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:40.540
You already saw the
dashboard that we showed you

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:43.410
a bit ago from Looker.

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:47.560
Looker is a great tool, it has
excellent support for BigQuery,

00:14:47.560 --> 00:14:51.730
and is really very nice to
work with as I can attest.

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:56.170
But it's not the only option you
have for visualizing your data.

00:14:56.170 --> 00:14:57.970
Google Data Studio
is built directly

00:14:57.970 --> 00:14:59.400
into the Cloud platform.

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:00.310
It is free to use.

00:15:00.310 --> 00:15:02.590
It's a great way to
really democratize

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:05.530
the ability of people
in your organization

00:15:05.530 --> 00:15:08.870
to create visualizations
and share that out

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:10.780
to the rest of the organization.

00:15:10.780 --> 00:15:14.620
And there's a long list of
other BI and visualization tools

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:16.285
that are supported
by our partners.

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:22.880
I talked about the
data engineer's need

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:26.450
to be able to reprocess
data and maybe

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:29.820
go back and recalculate things.

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:34.630
The Cloud Storage archive
is what enables this.

00:15:34.630 --> 00:15:40.540
So having stored our events
in an archive on Cloud Storage

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:45.340
as a bunch of Avro files, should
a data engineer need to do so,

00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:48.610
we can write another
pipeline that might go back

00:15:48.610 --> 00:15:51.490
and reprocess all of that
data out of Cloud Storage

00:15:51.490 --> 00:15:56.050
before writing it again
to our eventual sync.

00:15:56.050 --> 00:15:59.200
This is usually run as something
like a batch job, in contrast

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:02.500
to the streaming pipeline
that you saw earlier.

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:05.620
What's great about this
framework, and Cloud Dataflow

00:16:05.620 --> 00:16:09.010
in particular, is
that if I fix my bug

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:12.730
or change my business rules
in my streaming pipeline,

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:15.850
those transforms that I used
for the streaming pipeline can

00:16:15.850 --> 00:16:18.220
usually be used pretty
much directly in a batch

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:20.530
pipeline that runs along side.

00:16:20.530 --> 00:16:23.390
This is how we enable
the reprocessing of data,

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:25.375
the recalculation,
restatement of results.

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:33.481
When you run a batch pipeline,
it's not always the case

00:16:33.481 --> 00:16:35.730
that the batch pipeline that
you're looking to execute

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:37.380
is the only thing
that needs to run.

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:40.429
You usually need some
way to orchestrate this.

00:16:40.429 --> 00:16:41.970
There might be
dependencies that have

00:16:41.970 --> 00:16:46.140
to be satisfied before
executing the batch pipeline.

00:16:46.140 --> 00:16:48.960
Creating a database,
creating a table,

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:51.120
executing a different
Dataflow job,

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:54.150
or executing some bash script.

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:56.220
We recently announced
the general availability

00:16:56.220 --> 00:17:00.740
of Cloud Composer which is
based on Apache Airflow.

00:17:00.740 --> 00:17:06.099
Apache Airflow is a
service for orchestrating

00:17:06.099 --> 00:17:09.790
complex data pipelines,
particularly of the batch style

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:12.609
that I'm describing here.

00:17:12.609 --> 00:17:16.210
Cloud Composer is a
managed service for Airflow

00:17:16.210 --> 00:17:20.050
and makes it really easy to
set up and manage and run

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:21.010
an Airflow cluster.

00:17:24.099 --> 00:17:26.140
Now that I've taken you
through the architecture,

00:17:26.140 --> 00:17:28.280
I'd love to welcome
KaYun back to the stage

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:30.140
to give you a little
bit of a deeper dive

00:17:30.140 --> 00:17:31.975
into some of our
architecture choices.

00:17:31.975 --> 00:17:34.100
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:34.100 --> 00:17:37.110
KAYUN LAM: Thank you, Vince.

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:38.080
All right.

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:41.190
Thank you Vince for guiding
us through the modern data

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:42.760
pipeline in action.

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:46.590
So I would like to go back
and revisit it, and then

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:49.320
take a look at some of
the architecture decisions

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:53.100
that we have made when
presenting this solution.

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:56.580
Of course, just now
the example is a game.

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:59.350
In your use case, it may
or may not be a game.

00:17:59.350 --> 00:18:02.220
It could be in a traditional
enterprise environment.

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:05.710
It could be some other kinds
of data processing needs.

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:07.996
We still want to
revisit or maybe go

00:18:07.996 --> 00:18:09.870
through some of those
architectural decisions

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:12.180
to see if there's
something universal.

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:14.680
Maybe there's something that
you can borrow and apply

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:17.297
in your data pipeline
in your environment.

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:23.960
First of all, just
now the data flows

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:27.240
from Firebase, the
application that you

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:29.990
used your phone to play on.

00:18:29.990 --> 00:18:33.110
And then eventually
it goes into BigQuery

00:18:33.110 --> 00:18:35.820
for the dashboard
for the analytics.

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:39.330
Technically speaking, it is
actually doable, technically

00:18:39.330 --> 00:18:43.890
feasible, to have the
data written from Firebase

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:47.180
into BigQuery directly.

00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:49.120
As a matter of fact,
BigQuery actually

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:51.750
supports multiple ways
of ingesting data.

00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:55.010
BigQuery can ingest
data in batch.

00:18:55.010 --> 00:18:56.990
BigQuery, on the
other hand, also

00:18:56.990 --> 00:19:00.110
has a streaming
insert mechanism.

00:19:00.110 --> 00:19:02.570
That means when you
run a simple statement,

00:19:02.570 --> 00:19:05.480
it actually can merge the
data, merge the results

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:08.840
between a streaming buffer
in BigQuery with the data

00:19:08.840 --> 00:19:11.420
that it has been presented
in the table already.

00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:13.520
So it is actually
very, very doable

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:17.470
to have Firebase to write the
data to BigQuery directly.

00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:19.640
We choose not to do that.

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:22.310
We chose to put
something in between,

00:19:22.310 --> 00:19:25.970
and that is what
we call decoupling.

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:28.280
In this case, we want the
application-- in this case,

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:31.250
in this example,
Firebase-- to focus only

00:19:31.250 --> 00:19:34.190
on publishing the source
data, the source data

00:19:34.190 --> 00:19:37.110
from the application
in its original format.

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:39.260
In this case, it is JSON data.

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:43.040
And just focuses on publishing
the data onto the Cloud Pub/Sub

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:44.620
topic.

00:19:44.620 --> 00:19:46.820
The application doesn't
need to be aware of,

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:49.060
hey, what is the data
set name down the road.

00:19:49.060 --> 00:19:52.090
Hey, where is BigQuery
or some other databases?

00:19:52.090 --> 00:19:53.470
What is the data set name?

00:19:53.470 --> 00:19:54.520
What is the table name?

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:57.010
How the schema looks like.

00:19:57.010 --> 00:20:01.600
And then in many cases, it may
not be one single downstream

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:02.740
application.

00:20:02.740 --> 00:20:05.730
Right now it could be one, it
could be a second one later.

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:06.920
And it can grow.

00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:09.100
And then we don't
want to have so many

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:11.800
of those touch points
built into the source

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:13.930
application in this case.

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:16.000
Having Pub/Sub in this
case will make sure

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:18.820
that Firebase, the
source application,

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:20.820
only focuses on
publishing the data.

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:26.380
And it will let Pub/Sub be the
mechanism to fan out the data,

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:31.200
to have multiple subscribers
subscribing to the same topic.

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:34.650
Each Cloud Pub/Sub
topic supports up

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:37.260
to 10,000 subscribers.

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:39.210
You don't need to
handle to design

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:42.570
your own fan out mechanism,
Pub/Sub can do it for you.

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:50.870
The concept of message
[? bus, ?] service [? bus, ?]

00:20:50.870 --> 00:20:53.720
this is not new, to be honest.

00:20:53.720 --> 00:20:55.070
It has been around.

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:57.620
People have talked
about a message layer,

00:20:57.620 --> 00:21:01.090
message queue, stream storage.

00:21:01.090 --> 00:21:02.800
This is not new, to be honest.

00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:05.910
But at the same
time, it has been

00:21:05.910 --> 00:21:07.840
a little bit
challenging to architect

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:10.887
this kind of solution.

00:21:10.887 --> 00:21:12.470
Issues that you may
have heard around,

00:21:12.470 --> 00:21:16.420
message is stuck in a queue, the
processing cannot keep up with

00:21:16.420 --> 00:21:20.020
the incoming data volume,
or if you are lucky to have

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:22.840
a solution that is
horizontally scalable,

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:26.020
it has multiple
partitions, multiple shots,

00:21:26.020 --> 00:21:28.530
then you need to worry
about how should I shot it,

00:21:28.530 --> 00:21:30.360
how should I partition it?

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:33.370
The source application may
need to calculate the shotting,

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:35.740
make sure that it is
evenly distributed.

00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:38.500
If it is not evenly
distributed, how do you

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:41.980
split the shot to rebalance it?

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:45.020
It becomes the architecture
exercise on its own

00:21:45.020 --> 00:21:49.010
to deliver this kind
of message solution.

00:21:49.010 --> 00:21:51.730
In this case, in our
example, Cloud Pub/Sub

00:21:51.730 --> 00:21:54.420
is a fully managed
service running

00:21:54.420 --> 00:21:56.210
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:21:56.210 --> 00:21:58.240
It is a global service.

00:21:58.240 --> 00:22:01.520
That's one single end
point around the globe.

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:03.930
Meaning that when you are
publishing your messages

00:22:03.930 --> 00:22:06.450
to the topic, you don't
need to worry about, hm,

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:12.160
am I publishing my messages
to US, to Europe, to Asia?

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:15.150
No, you're publishing
your messages to Pub/Sub

00:22:15.150 --> 00:22:18.000
and Pub/Sub will
handle the rest.

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:20.910
You don't need to worry
about which partitioning

00:22:20.910 --> 00:22:24.040
or which shot you're
publishing your messages to.

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:26.990
Cloud Pub/Sub will just
scale automatically for you

00:22:26.990 --> 00:22:29.820
and you are just paying
for that data volume

00:22:29.820 --> 00:22:33.670
that you're sending
to Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:22:33.670 --> 00:22:36.380
The processing layer,
Cloud Dataflow,

00:22:36.380 --> 00:22:39.730
it reads data from Pub/Sub.

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:43.420
There could be varying
volume of data coming in

00:22:43.420 --> 00:22:46.650
throughout the day, throughout
the week, holiday season.

00:22:46.650 --> 00:22:49.090
There are many of those
similar processing

00:22:49.090 --> 00:22:52.330
framework can do something
like this horizontally scalable

00:22:52.330 --> 00:22:53.520
manner.

00:22:53.520 --> 00:22:55.780
But at the same time,
that horizontal factor

00:22:55.780 --> 00:22:57.580
usually is a fixed factor.

00:22:57.580 --> 00:23:01.270
Meaning that you design it
upfront whether it is four

00:23:01.270 --> 00:23:04.420
[? nodes, ?] 10 [? nodes, ?]
10,000 [? nodes. ?] You have

00:23:04.420 --> 00:23:08.020
to have a fixed set of computing
power in order to consume

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:09.790
the messages.

00:23:09.790 --> 00:23:12.010
On the other hand,
Cloud Dataflow

00:23:12.010 --> 00:23:15.040
can actually do something
like auto scaling.

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:16.690
Based on the
incoming data volume

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:19.600
coming from streaming or
maybe even from batch,

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:23.800
Cloud Dataflow has the ability
to scale up and scale down

00:23:23.800 --> 00:23:25.750
the computing
resources underneath.

00:23:25.750 --> 00:23:28.750
So that you can adjust it and
only pay for the resources

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:31.160
that you are truly consuming.

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:34.390
There's no need to over
provision your streaming

00:23:34.390 --> 00:23:38.550
processing layer just to
cater for the high water mark.

00:23:38.550 --> 00:23:40.740
And then in this
case, the destination

00:23:40.740 --> 00:23:45.930
of the data BigQuery, it has
compute storage separation.

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:48.660
Meaning that when the
data volume grows,

00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:50.630
you don't need to
necessarily tie

00:23:50.630 --> 00:23:53.130
the storage cost with
the computing costs,

00:23:53.130 --> 00:23:55.110
like the CPU and the memory.

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:59.310
You pay for the storage and
the processing separately.

00:23:59.310 --> 00:24:02.880
This makes it simple,
elastic, and low cost.

00:24:06.530 --> 00:24:09.320
When we are sending
data to the destination

00:24:09.320 --> 00:24:13.640
under the consideration, under
the architecture decision is,

00:24:13.640 --> 00:24:17.460
how do you want that
data to look like?

00:24:17.460 --> 00:24:20.520
Traditionally speaking, I've
been in that kind of field,

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:25.260
like ETL jobs, trying to
fit into the data schema

00:24:25.260 --> 00:24:27.000
on the data warehouse.

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:30.000
All those things are
simple as normalization

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:34.520
of the data, the star schema,
slowly changing dimension.

00:24:34.520 --> 00:24:38.130
Just all those ETL jobs,
they are not really

00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:41.190
doing something that is
business wise, but more to cater

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:44.070
for the target database format.

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:46.360
Usually the goal is
to fit into the schema

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:49.170
so that it is efficient
for that database queries,

00:24:49.170 --> 00:24:51.390
or maybe it will
minimize the data storage

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:54.360
so that you're minimizing the
duplication, saving the storage

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:56.080
cost.

00:24:56.080 --> 00:24:58.900
On the other hand, in
a modern data pipeline,

00:24:58.900 --> 00:25:04.030
we can ease off on some of these
transformations in between.

00:25:04.030 --> 00:25:06.910
The reason is that in a modern
data pipeline, especially

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:10.530
for example in this case,
what we have in BigQuery,

00:25:10.530 --> 00:25:14.070
you don't have to force it to be
like a one-to-many relationship

00:25:14.070 --> 00:25:17.260
table to split up the
data into multiple places.

00:25:17.260 --> 00:25:20.560
In a modern data pipeline, a
storage system like BigQuery

00:25:20.560 --> 00:25:22.990
when we're [? top ?]
storing the structure data,

00:25:22.990 --> 00:25:26.080
you have the ability to
store it in a format that

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:29.290
is as close to the
source as possible.

00:25:29.290 --> 00:25:30.972
In this case, there
could be arrays,

00:25:30.972 --> 00:25:32.430
there could be
[? strokes, ?] there

00:25:32.430 --> 00:25:36.920
could be complex data types in
the originating source system.

00:25:36.920 --> 00:25:40.990
And you can persist the data
as close to the source format

00:25:40.990 --> 00:25:42.910
as possible so
that you don't need

00:25:42.910 --> 00:25:45.580
to worry about should I break
down the data into this table?

00:25:45.580 --> 00:25:46.270
That table?

00:25:46.270 --> 00:25:47.950
How does the [? EL ?]
diagram look like?

00:25:47.950 --> 00:25:49.840
And things like that.

00:25:49.840 --> 00:25:53.750
This allows the data
processing to be really fast.

00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:55.780
We minimize the logic,
minimize anything

00:25:55.780 --> 00:25:57.700
that may happen in
between, and now we

00:25:57.700 --> 00:26:01.300
can have low latency
access to the data.

00:26:01.300 --> 00:26:02.090
That is our goal.

00:26:05.050 --> 00:26:07.120
And the example we
had just now, we

00:26:07.120 --> 00:26:11.667
showed a streaming
example of us in the room,

00:26:11.667 --> 00:26:13.750
playing with the phone,
and then sending that data

00:26:13.750 --> 00:26:16.710
so that is streaming data.

00:26:16.710 --> 00:26:19.080
In real life situation,
that streaming

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:21.690
may not be the only data source.

00:26:21.690 --> 00:26:24.900
Usually you will be handling
a mixture of streaming data

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:28.090
sources and batch data sources.

00:26:28.090 --> 00:26:29.490
They could be the
same data type.

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:30.880
They might be different.

00:26:30.880 --> 00:26:33.690
So it is very, very important
architecture decision

00:26:33.690 --> 00:26:36.840
to choose something that
you are not necessarily

00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:39.900
writing two separate
data pipelines or two

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:42.330
separate pieces of codes.

00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:46.920
Cloud Dataflow supports
both streaming and batch.

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:49.740
Meaning that when you're using
the underlying programming

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:52.950
model, you write the set
of transformation logic,

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:55.480
you write something,
some algorithm.

00:26:55.480 --> 00:26:59.130
It's very, very easy to have
that same piece of logic

00:26:59.130 --> 00:27:04.200
to work on streaming
data and also batch data.

00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:07.230
So keep this in mind when you're
designing your data pipeline

00:27:07.230 --> 00:27:10.395
to make sure that it is flexible
enough to handle both cases.

00:27:12.950 --> 00:27:15.320
One use case, as Vince
mentioned earlier,

00:27:15.320 --> 00:27:17.920
is on the reprocessing needs.

00:27:17.920 --> 00:27:21.220
It could be based on changing
business requirements

00:27:21.220 --> 00:27:24.040
or you have to go back
and fix something.

00:27:24.040 --> 00:27:29.254
But very, very often, data is
a very, very valuable asset.

00:27:29.254 --> 00:27:30.670
There are so many
cases that there

00:27:30.670 --> 00:27:33.670
might be some insights
hidden in the raw data

00:27:33.670 --> 00:27:37.150
that maybe in the future maybe
there's an upcoming analytics

00:27:37.150 --> 00:27:40.000
project, someone in
your organization

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:43.174
just want to go back to
the data and take a look

00:27:43.174 --> 00:27:45.340
and see if there's some
additional insights that you

00:27:45.340 --> 00:27:49.360
can get out of the raw
data that could have

00:27:49.360 --> 00:27:52.390
come in months or years ago.

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:55.150
So it is also very
important when

00:27:55.150 --> 00:27:59.040
you are designing your data
pipeline to keep reprocessing

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:00.850
as one of the considerations.

00:28:00.850 --> 00:28:04.420
You want to make it easy for
anyone in your organization

00:28:04.420 --> 00:28:06.460
to just go back and say,
hey, I have an idea.

00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:08.020
I want to take a
look at the old data

00:28:08.020 --> 00:28:09.980
and see if there's a
certain kind of pattern,

00:28:09.980 --> 00:28:12.760
and then I can come up
with this new prediction

00:28:12.760 --> 00:28:17.480
algorithm which has to be
trained based on old data.

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:21.820
That's why reprocessing, having
this as architecture decision,

00:28:21.820 --> 00:28:23.410
is important.

00:28:23.410 --> 00:28:27.100
As supported in my previous
slide stream and batch,

00:28:27.100 --> 00:28:31.270
reprocessing can take
advantage of stream and batch

00:28:31.270 --> 00:28:34.790
capabilities in the Cloud
Dataflow's programming model.

00:28:34.790 --> 00:28:38.842
And then you can go back and
process the existing data

00:28:38.842 --> 00:28:41.300
and see what kind of additional
insights there you can get.

00:28:44.500 --> 00:28:48.350
So here is kind of like a
flow chart on the high level

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:53.650
transformation that might exist
in a Cloud Dataflow pipeline.

00:28:53.650 --> 00:28:55.970
In this example,
first of all, first

00:28:55.970 --> 00:28:58.750
of the four boxes
over there, first box

00:28:58.750 --> 00:29:02.860
reads from the Cloud Pub/Sub
topic and subscription.

00:29:02.860 --> 00:29:07.010
Second passes the JSON message.

00:29:07.010 --> 00:29:11.740
The third step is to turn it
into the BigQuery, the table

00:29:11.740 --> 00:29:13.180
row format.

00:29:13.180 --> 00:29:17.800
And then the fourth step
is to write to BigQuery.

00:29:17.800 --> 00:29:21.430
You want to choose the
programming model that allows

00:29:21.430 --> 00:29:23.890
you to express your pipeline.

00:29:23.890 --> 00:29:27.020
And these are kind of
high level transformations

00:29:27.020 --> 00:29:32.880
so you're not going to deal with
all those tiny, little details.

00:29:32.880 --> 00:29:34.400
The Dataflow's
programming models

00:29:34.400 --> 00:29:37.420
supports many other primitives
that are specialized

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:40.030
for streaming data processing.

00:29:40.030 --> 00:29:42.540
Like a windowing
mechanism, for example,

00:29:42.540 --> 00:29:46.780
a sliding window, fixed
window, session window.

00:29:46.780 --> 00:29:49.030
So it is very
important when you're

00:29:49.030 --> 00:29:53.230
designing your data pipeline
next time, keep this in mind.

00:29:53.230 --> 00:29:56.410
Choose something that allows
you to express your pipeline

00:29:56.410 --> 00:30:01.540
in these high-level primitives.

00:30:01.540 --> 00:30:03.250
And when you are
designing something

00:30:03.250 --> 00:30:05.980
to be expressed in
a high level manner,

00:30:05.980 --> 00:30:09.710
it allows your data
pipelines to be portable.

00:30:09.710 --> 00:30:14.170
Just now I've been talking
about Cloud Dataflow.

00:30:14.170 --> 00:30:17.290
In fact, it supports
pipelines that

00:30:17.290 --> 00:30:21.640
are written in Apache Beam SDK.

00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:24.610
But Cloud Dataflow is
not the only option.

00:30:24.610 --> 00:30:26.590
The Cloud Dataflow
does make it easier

00:30:26.590 --> 00:30:29.890
to run your Apache
Beam data pipelines.

00:30:29.890 --> 00:30:32.080
It proficients the
computing resources for you.

00:30:32.080 --> 00:30:33.670
It scales up, scales down.

00:30:33.670 --> 00:30:37.450
It rebalances the work like
what we have mentioned earlier.

00:30:37.450 --> 00:30:41.730
But if you want to choose
to run it somewhere else,

00:30:41.730 --> 00:30:43.020
you can do that.

00:30:43.020 --> 00:30:47.370
Once you have written your
data pipeline in Apache Beam,

00:30:47.370 --> 00:30:53.010
Cloud Dataflow is just one of
the several supported runners

00:30:53.010 --> 00:30:54.070
behind the scene.

00:30:54.070 --> 00:30:58.745
You can say, I want to run my
data pipeline on Apache Spark.

00:30:58.745 --> 00:31:03.230
It could be on Apache
Apax, Flink, Gearpump,

00:31:03.230 --> 00:31:06.900
Samza, or even locally when
you're doing development

00:31:06.900 --> 00:31:08.540
on your workstation.

00:31:08.540 --> 00:31:11.220
So when you're designing
your data pipeline,

00:31:11.220 --> 00:31:14.350
you want to choose something
open so that on one hand,

00:31:14.350 --> 00:31:18.480
you can run it on, for example,
Cloud Dataflow on a cloud.

00:31:18.480 --> 00:31:20.880
On the other hand, if there
are some other requirements,

00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:24.350
you can choose to run it on
your own premise environment

00:31:24.350 --> 00:31:25.689
or some other environments.

00:31:25.689 --> 00:31:26.980
There shouldn't be any lock in.

00:31:29.800 --> 00:31:33.590
So with this said, I'll pass
the time to my colleague Julie

00:31:33.590 --> 00:31:36.310
and then she'll explain to
you the customer stories--

00:31:36.310 --> 00:31:38.790
the customers who are
running these modern data

00:31:38.790 --> 00:31:40.890
pipelines on Google
Cloud Platform.

00:31:40.890 --> 00:31:41.770
Thank you, Julie.

00:31:41.770 --> 00:31:43.730
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:45.690 --> 00:31:47.850
JULIE PRICE: Hello, everybody.

00:31:47.850 --> 00:31:50.370
Thanks KaYun and thanks
Vince for walking us

00:31:50.370 --> 00:31:52.740
through the concepts of
a modern data pipeline

00:31:52.740 --> 00:31:55.440
and the architectural
decisions that go into creating

00:31:55.440 --> 00:31:57.180
a modern data pipeline.

00:31:57.180 --> 00:31:59.610
As KaYun mentioned, I
wanted to take a moment

00:31:59.610 --> 00:32:03.510
to really make it real to talk
about how some companies are

00:32:03.510 --> 00:32:07.850
creating modern data
pipelines of their own in GCP.

00:32:07.850 --> 00:32:10.000
So all of the companies
that you see listed here

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:11.800
are doing some
really cool things

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:15.070
with big data analytics,
big data processing,

00:32:15.070 --> 00:32:17.710
all with Google Cloud Services.

00:32:17.710 --> 00:32:20.810
And some of them, most of them,
you've already heard about.

00:32:20.810 --> 00:32:23.710
So the first customer I
wanted to talk about is Ocado.

00:32:23.710 --> 00:32:25.630
Ocado is an online
grocery retailer.

00:32:25.630 --> 00:32:29.650
They serve over 70% of
households within the UK.

00:32:29.650 --> 00:32:31.900
They don't have a single
brick and mortar store

00:32:31.900 --> 00:32:33.430
anywhere in the UK.

00:32:33.430 --> 00:32:36.850
So they're competing with all
of these really large stores

00:32:36.850 --> 00:32:39.209
that people go past
on their way home.

00:32:39.209 --> 00:32:40.750
And so they really
need to find a way

00:32:40.750 --> 00:32:43.690
to get their
customers to be loyal,

00:32:43.690 --> 00:32:46.310
to make sure that they're
always purchasing through them.

00:32:46.310 --> 00:32:50.230
So what they wanted to do was
create a big data analytics

00:32:50.230 --> 00:32:54.160
platform which would help
them convince customers

00:32:54.160 --> 00:32:56.590
of all of the benefits
of buying online

00:32:56.590 --> 00:32:58.820
as opposed to in the stores.

00:32:58.820 --> 00:33:00.520
And they also
wanted to make sure

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:03.520
that they were using data to
drive their business insights,

00:33:03.520 --> 00:33:05.620
to do things like
inform the supply chain,

00:33:05.620 --> 00:33:08.470
predict demand, and really
just overall improve

00:33:08.470 --> 00:33:09.790
their logistics.

00:33:09.790 --> 00:33:12.700
They had a problem because
their data was siloed.

00:33:12.700 --> 00:33:16.220
They had business
data and product data.

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:17.770
Transactional data
was all sitting

00:33:17.770 --> 00:33:19.840
in different places
across their data center,

00:33:19.840 --> 00:33:22.672
and they had no means of
communicating with each other.

00:33:22.672 --> 00:33:24.130
So what they knew
they needed to do

00:33:24.130 --> 00:33:27.190
was come up with a way to
create a platform that could

00:33:27.190 --> 00:33:29.170
pull all this data together.

00:33:29.170 --> 00:33:31.317
And they did that in GCP.

00:33:31.317 --> 00:33:33.400
So they were able to build
this advanced analytics

00:33:33.400 --> 00:33:37.430
solution that could not only
process the data through,

00:33:37.430 --> 00:33:39.130
but run advanced
analytics against it

00:33:39.130 --> 00:33:42.280
to do things like determine
what would be best to improve

00:33:42.280 --> 00:33:45.970
customer satisfaction, how they
could optimize their supply

00:33:45.970 --> 00:33:49.480
chain, how they could really
get access to insights

00:33:49.480 --> 00:33:52.690
about their business data
in closer to real time,

00:33:52.690 --> 00:33:54.740
and ultimately reduce costs.

00:33:54.740 --> 00:33:56.690
So all of those kind of
common business goals

00:33:56.690 --> 00:33:59.860
that almost every company has.

00:33:59.860 --> 00:34:03.220
So they did that by implementing
first and foremost BigQuery

00:34:03.220 --> 00:34:05.320
as their data storage platform.

00:34:05.320 --> 00:34:09.430
So BigQuery is where all of the
data ends up to be analyzed.

00:34:09.430 --> 00:34:12.489
So they do click stream
analysis, customer analysis,

00:34:12.489 --> 00:34:14.840
product and department analysis.

00:34:14.840 --> 00:34:17.530
Everything you can think of
all happening in BigQuery.

00:34:17.530 --> 00:34:21.500
And this is across over
two petabytes of data.

00:34:21.500 --> 00:34:23.889
They also are using
Cloud Dataflow

00:34:23.889 --> 00:34:25.460
for doing their transformations.

00:34:25.460 --> 00:34:28.060
So you might remember Vince was
talking about Dataflow quite

00:34:28.060 --> 00:34:29.270
a bit as well.

00:34:29.270 --> 00:34:32.739
And then they're using Pub/Sub
for all of their data delivery.

00:34:32.739 --> 00:34:36.239
So it's this kind of a common
pattern of Pub/Sub, Dataflow,

00:34:36.239 --> 00:34:37.920
to BigQuery.

00:34:37.920 --> 00:34:41.800
And now they took it even a step
further by integrating BigQuery

00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:44.949
directly with TensorFlow and
Cloud Machine Learning Engine.

00:34:44.949 --> 00:34:46.725
Ocado was one of the
very first customers

00:34:46.725 --> 00:34:49.840
to even help us test the
Cloud Machine Learning Engine.

00:34:49.840 --> 00:34:52.540
And what they did was create
these really awesome data

00:34:52.540 --> 00:34:55.270
pipelines that do
very real things.

00:34:55.270 --> 00:34:57.040
So one example of
that is the way

00:34:57.040 --> 00:34:59.200
that when somebody
submits an order,

00:34:59.200 --> 00:35:01.852
the items get picked and
packed and ready for delivery.

00:35:01.852 --> 00:35:04.310
So let's say, I'm going to make
a curry for dinner tonight.

00:35:04.310 --> 00:35:07.900
And I put into my
Ocado app that I

00:35:07.900 --> 00:35:10.840
want some vegetables, a
protein, some lentils, rice,

00:35:10.840 --> 00:35:12.720
and some nice naan bread.

00:35:12.720 --> 00:35:14.380
It's around noon time, right?

00:35:14.380 --> 00:35:16.390
I'm on my lunch, not
taking up my work time

00:35:16.390 --> 00:35:18.130
with ordering my groceries.

00:35:18.130 --> 00:35:20.890
And I go ahead and
enter that, and that's

00:35:20.890 --> 00:35:23.140
when the system starts
to work really well.

00:35:23.140 --> 00:35:25.840
Because now we have to
analyze where are these items?

00:35:25.840 --> 00:35:26.680
In what warehouse?

00:35:26.680 --> 00:35:30.910
What warehouse is closest to
where it needs to be delivered?

00:35:30.910 --> 00:35:32.170
Are there perishables?

00:35:32.170 --> 00:35:33.310
What can be picked now?

00:35:33.310 --> 00:35:35.180
What has to be picked later?

00:35:35.180 --> 00:35:37.360
And so it determines
the appropriate time

00:35:37.360 --> 00:35:40.360
to do these things and sends
signals to the warehouses.

00:35:40.360 --> 00:35:42.460
And the warehouses
have a number of robots

00:35:42.460 --> 00:35:48.010
that are used to pick all of the
items in an automated fashion.

00:35:48.010 --> 00:35:50.620
But they just don't
say, hey you, robot,

00:35:50.620 --> 00:35:52.780
you are responsible
for Julie's order.

00:35:52.780 --> 00:35:55.420
In fact, there's ML
swarm intelligence

00:35:55.420 --> 00:35:58.000
that they've created
which will help the robots

00:35:58.000 --> 00:35:59.680
work collaboratively
so that they

00:35:59.680 --> 00:36:00.910
can go through the warehouse.

00:36:00.910 --> 00:36:04.810
And many robots could be working
on picking my items, as well

00:36:04.810 --> 00:36:06.820
as others, but making
sure that they all

00:36:06.820 --> 00:36:09.130
end up in the right
package to be shipped.

00:36:09.130 --> 00:36:11.320
And making sure that it's
all done in such a way

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:14.440
that everything ends up
on people's doors fresh as

00:36:14.440 --> 00:36:17.050
could be with no perishables.

00:36:17.050 --> 00:36:19.660
So it is really pretty
cool what they've done.

00:36:19.660 --> 00:36:22.870
Even still, they're sending
all of the telemetry data

00:36:22.870 --> 00:36:24.670
from the robots up to
the Cloud and they're

00:36:24.670 --> 00:36:27.340
analyzing that so they can
do scheduling, to make sure

00:36:27.340 --> 00:36:28.930
they have the right
number of robots

00:36:28.930 --> 00:36:30.944
on the floor in each warehouse.

00:36:30.944 --> 00:36:32.485
And to do things
like predictive wear

00:36:32.485 --> 00:36:36.160
and tear, to make sure that if
they think that something might

00:36:36.160 --> 00:36:38.260
happen to a robot, that
can pull it and replace it

00:36:38.260 --> 00:36:40.450
with a well-working
robot before something

00:36:40.450 --> 00:36:43.520
happens and interrupts
the workflow.

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:46.090
So that's what they've
done internally

00:36:46.090 --> 00:36:48.400
using this modern data pipeline.

00:36:48.400 --> 00:36:51.700
And they also determined that--
they do a really good job

00:36:51.700 --> 00:36:52.545
with e-commerce.

00:36:52.545 --> 00:36:53.920
And so they thought,
why don't we

00:36:53.920 --> 00:36:57.040
create an e-commerce
platform that we

00:36:57.040 --> 00:36:58.970
can sell to other customers?

00:36:58.970 --> 00:37:00.820
So large brick and
mortars that also do

00:37:00.820 --> 00:37:04.600
online, and they created
Ocado Smart Platform,

00:37:04.600 --> 00:37:07.480
which leverages a lot of
these same types of pipelines

00:37:07.480 --> 00:37:11.770
to ensure now that these other
retailers across the globe

00:37:11.770 --> 00:37:15.220
can deliver the same type of
fantastic e-commerce experience

00:37:15.220 --> 00:37:18.870
that Ocado does to
their customers.

00:37:18.870 --> 00:37:20.970
Now, the next customer
that I wanted to talk about

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:21.553
is Brightcove.

00:37:21.553 --> 00:37:23.670
So a very different company.

00:37:23.670 --> 00:37:26.910
They're actually an
online video platform.

00:37:26.910 --> 00:37:28.920
They serve content
for internet TV,

00:37:28.920 --> 00:37:31.050
news media, various
different internet media.

00:37:31.050 --> 00:37:33.360
So think all video streaming.

00:37:33.360 --> 00:37:41.040
And Brightcove runs 8,500
years worth of video streaming,

00:37:41.040 --> 00:37:43.290
video viewing each
and every month.

00:37:43.290 --> 00:37:46.459
They have 7 billion
analytic events per day.

00:37:46.459 --> 00:37:47.500
So that's a lot of data--

00:37:47.500 --> 00:37:49.950
85,000 years worth
of video streaming

00:37:49.950 --> 00:37:53.250
per month for all the
people that are watching.

00:37:53.250 --> 00:37:54.750
And so they were
having a problem

00:37:54.750 --> 00:37:58.530
because their legacy system was
kind of bursting at the seams.

00:37:58.530 --> 00:38:01.440
They knew that they needed to
re-architect and re-platform.

00:38:01.440 --> 00:38:03.900
And so they investigated a
number of different big data

00:38:03.900 --> 00:38:06.690
stacks, and they
landed upon GCP.

00:38:06.690 --> 00:38:07.740
And so what did they do?

00:38:07.740 --> 00:38:09.480
They implemented
a data flow that

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:12.870
went from Pub/Sub for
event delivery, Dataflow

00:38:12.870 --> 00:38:15.510
for all of their
transformations, and BigQuery

00:38:15.510 --> 00:38:19.000
to land their data and be
able to perform analytics.

00:38:19.000 --> 00:38:21.510
And they chose this because
all of those services

00:38:21.510 --> 00:38:24.242
can scale to whatever scale that
they need without them having

00:38:24.242 --> 00:38:26.700
to worry about whether or not
the infrastructure can handle

00:38:26.700 --> 00:38:27.720
it.

00:38:27.720 --> 00:38:30.084
So this story's a little bit
shorter than the last one,

00:38:30.084 --> 00:38:31.500
but really interesting
nonetheless

00:38:31.500 --> 00:38:34.770
just because of the grand scale
with the amount of information

00:38:34.770 --> 00:38:37.710
that moves through the
pipeline every single day

00:38:37.710 --> 00:38:39.600
and the amount of
information that gets

00:38:39.600 --> 00:38:42.400
analyzed every single day.

00:38:42.400 --> 00:38:45.734
And speaking of scale, that
brings us to our last customer

00:38:45.734 --> 00:38:46.900
that I'd like to talk about.

00:38:46.900 --> 00:38:50.340
So are there any
Spotify users in here?

00:38:50.340 --> 00:38:51.220
A few.

00:38:51.220 --> 00:38:52.900
So I personally use
Spotify every day.

00:38:52.900 --> 00:38:54.150
I absolutely love the service.

00:38:54.150 --> 00:38:58.470
So I was very excited to learn
that Spotify chose to put

00:38:58.470 --> 00:39:00.589
all of their tech stack on GCP.

00:39:00.589 --> 00:39:02.880
So we're not going to talk
about all of the tech stack,

00:39:02.880 --> 00:39:05.255
we're just going to talk about
the cool stuff, of course,

00:39:05.255 --> 00:39:07.320
the data stuff today.

00:39:07.320 --> 00:39:12.450
Spotify had the largest Hadoop
environments in Europe--

00:39:12.450 --> 00:39:16.650
a 2,500 node, 50,000 CPU
core Hadoop environment

00:39:16.650 --> 00:39:21.324
with 100 petabytes of
capacity, 100 terabytes of RAM.

00:39:21.324 --> 00:39:22.740
It makes me shudder
at the thought

00:39:22.740 --> 00:39:25.260
of the expense of that system.

00:39:25.260 --> 00:39:28.530
But it was really important
because over 20,000 jobs were

00:39:28.530 --> 00:39:30.840
running on the system per day.

00:39:30.840 --> 00:39:32.700
From 2,000 different
workflows, it

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:35.490
was supporting 100 different
teams within Spotify.

00:39:35.490 --> 00:39:38.304
So you have to imagine
it was very complex.

00:39:38.304 --> 00:39:39.220
It was very important.

00:39:39.220 --> 00:39:43.380
They couldn't just break it down
and build it somewhere else.

00:39:43.380 --> 00:39:46.140
So they knew that they wanted
to get out of the on-prem world

00:39:46.140 --> 00:39:48.810
and out of the single
Hadoop cluster world.

00:39:48.810 --> 00:39:51.810
And so they chose
to come to GCP.

00:39:51.810 --> 00:39:54.634
And so maybe you might
recognize a pattern here.

00:39:54.634 --> 00:39:57.300
What do you think they chose for
their ad hoc analytics and data

00:39:57.300 --> 00:39:59.100
storage?

00:39:59.100 --> 00:40:00.600
They chose BigQuery.

00:40:00.600 --> 00:40:02.400
Their BigQuery
environment serves

00:40:02.400 --> 00:40:05.040
over 10 million queries
and scheduled jobs

00:40:05.040 --> 00:40:07.100
every single day--

00:40:07.100 --> 00:40:11.040
I'm sorry, every single month--
processes 500 petabytes of data

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:13.290
every single month for all
of the different users that

00:40:13.290 --> 00:40:16.020
need to query against it.

00:40:16.020 --> 00:40:18.720
Also, you might guess,
that for event delivery,

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:20.150
they chose Pub/Sub.

00:40:20.150 --> 00:40:22.650
And if you remember the scale
of what Brightcove was doing,

00:40:22.650 --> 00:40:27.300
7 billion analytic
events per day,

00:40:27.300 --> 00:40:30.300
Spotify has one
trillion requests

00:40:30.300 --> 00:40:32.250
that go against Pub/Sub.

00:40:32.250 --> 00:40:34.440
And Pub/Sub is able to
scale to handle that.

00:40:34.440 --> 00:40:37.682
Not only just to
handle it, but 99%

00:40:37.682 --> 00:40:39.390
of all requests that
come through Pub/Sub

00:40:39.390 --> 00:40:42.804
have a maximum latency
of 400 milliseconds.

00:40:42.804 --> 00:40:44.970
So I think they've got the
scale and the low latency

00:40:44.970 --> 00:40:48.460
pretty much worked out for
that part of the pipeline.

00:40:48.460 --> 00:40:51.029
Now data processing,
again you might have

00:40:51.029 --> 00:40:52.320
guessed they're using Dataflow.

00:40:52.320 --> 00:40:55.920
They run 5,000
Dataflow jobs per day.

00:40:55.920 --> 00:40:58.660
But they also realize
that they had some things

00:40:58.660 --> 00:41:00.810
that they wanted to
continue to do on Hadoop.

00:41:00.810 --> 00:41:02.880
So there was some
workloads, some ETL

00:41:02.880 --> 00:41:05.720
that they were doing in Hadoop
that they wanted to keep there.

00:41:05.720 --> 00:41:07.989
And so they also
introduced another service,

00:41:07.989 --> 00:41:09.780
which we haven't really
talked about today,

00:41:09.780 --> 00:41:11.100
which is Cloud Dataproc.

00:41:11.100 --> 00:41:15.432
And that's our managed Hadoop
environment within GCP.

00:41:15.432 --> 00:41:17.640
Well, the big difference
between a traditional Hadoop

00:41:17.640 --> 00:41:20.790
environment and Dataproc is that
we're able to take and decouple

00:41:20.790 --> 00:41:23.400
the storage from the
compute so now you

00:41:23.400 --> 00:41:26.120
can store your data in
Google Cloud Storage

00:41:26.120 --> 00:41:28.840
and just re-point
your Hadoop jobs

00:41:28.840 --> 00:41:32.230
to look at GCS instead of HDFS.

00:41:32.230 --> 00:41:33.930
And in doing that,
that enabled them

00:41:33.930 --> 00:41:37.200
to have very workflow specific
Hadoop environments that

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:40.466
only spin up when it's
time to run the job.

00:41:40.466 --> 00:41:42.090
They spin back down
when it's finished,

00:41:42.090 --> 00:41:44.040
and they only pay for
the compute resources

00:41:44.040 --> 00:41:46.520
when they absolutely need them.

00:41:46.520 --> 00:41:48.042
So you might have
seen, I'm not sure

00:41:48.042 --> 00:41:49.750
if anybody sat in this
session yesterday,

00:41:49.750 --> 00:41:52.090
but Spotify did a session
on their full migration

00:41:52.090 --> 00:41:55.810
from app tier all the
way down to the back end.

00:41:55.810 --> 00:41:57.550
So if interested, I
definitely recommend

00:41:57.550 --> 00:42:00.100
taking a look at that if you
want to see all of the thoughts

00:42:00.100 --> 00:42:02.500
and considerations they
went through when designing

00:42:02.500 --> 00:42:04.540
their solution in GCP.

00:42:04.540 --> 00:42:07.660
But the moral of the story, the
reason why I really bring it up

00:42:07.660 --> 00:42:12.460
today, is because it was huge
for Spotify to no longer have

00:42:12.460 --> 00:42:15.296
to worry about the
scale of infrastructure,

00:42:15.296 --> 00:42:16.670
the stretching of
infrastructure,

00:42:16.670 --> 00:42:18.336
whether or not they
had what they needed

00:42:18.336 --> 00:42:20.590
for the amount of data
that was coming in,

00:42:20.590 --> 00:42:23.020
or the growth that was
going to happen as they come

00:42:23.020 --> 00:42:27.560
with new music licensing and
new users coming to the system.

00:42:27.560 --> 00:42:30.100
Now they can just
analyze the data,

00:42:30.100 --> 00:42:32.530
understand listener
behaviors, understand

00:42:32.530 --> 00:42:35.260
how music tastes
correlate, and really build

00:42:35.260 --> 00:42:38.770
a fantastic music streaming
and music recommendation

00:42:38.770 --> 00:42:40.620
experience for the consumers.

